 {:seon.task/name "an-update-loses-nothing"
  :seon.task/plan "THE PLAN item 12. Owner's rulings (2026-11-15): an in-flight eval ends and resumes at its turn boundary from its branch (no root adopts another root's children); flights and requests are facts; nothing of ours is killed on wall time, only one spec'd stall dial for native code. So the design (audit target/root/audits-2026-11-15/processes-and-tests.md section 4.7): (a) each reconciler flight a fact {task step request} on main, re-issued idempotently by the next root (judges and verdicts reuse by digest); (b) a :running task of the old boot resumed, parked then woken (boot-wakes, load-diff!), never reaped :lost (reconcile.clj:870); (c) prove and :done requests facts keyed by run-key and task, so a client's re-ask after EOF reattaches; (d) the binary switch: build, boot the new binary on a clone, prove main through it, then stop the old root and start the new one; (e) one restart predicate (restart-for over baked-changes; loads-in-place? and build-input? collapse into it, with deps' runtime-for). Estimate to write per part before building; the proof below is the whole. THE PLAN M4, updates lose nothing (Stage 1's third safety item; root 2026-11-15). What a restart loses today [M, read in code, runtime-2 2026-11-15]: (1) every agent process: an agent's transport ends the process when root's socket end goes (seon.agent/await-reply: root gone ends the process); (2) the reconciler's in-memory :flights: a :running task comes back :lost, a model agent's work re-spawns on its branch, an outside agent's waits for a re-attach (seon.reconcile pass, the outside? clause); (3) every server op in flight: its connection drops, so a :done's answer is lost though its judge may still run. What a switch-over needs, none of which exists [M]: agents that outlive root's socket and reconnect to the next root (the socket is root's end of a socketpair the fork server passed); flights re-derived from the db at boot, not held only in memory (the judge's request, a spawn, a merge); clients that re-ask an op whose connection dropped. A pin then builds the next binary and switches to it with agents re-attaching, and the fork server loads only changed forms (seon.program/load-all!'s id-first path, root's half done in 7d4f5c9858). Deletes, when done: holds over restarts and the tree-compare stale-binary check (deps' runtime-for half). Done when: a pin lands during a watched seon_done and it ends green. Owner: runtime, deps for the binary's identity. Estimate and proofs to write before building. THE SEQUENCE (plan item 17; owner's ask via root 2026-11-15 14:2x: restart onto a new binary efficiently, losing nothing). Each step: what it reuses, what is measured, what is missing. MEASURED TODAY [root result/b3bcdc8d, d4b47f70, b497d653, ps]: 14 clean stops of root against 71 landings (a stop that overran and was killed writes no fact, so 14 is a floor); 5 followed a pin (Datahike 4, Jolt 1), 9 followed landings that changed no build input (6 of them 08:37-08:54); the restart's reason is printed by bin/update only and not kept (missing: a :seon.system/stopped fact carrying the reason, a one-key addition). The last restart: stop fact 13:29:31, new process 13:29:34; boot retain! (system.clj:1740, run when enough commits landed, before root serves, as the store's sole process) ran at 3 of 14 boots today at 47-57 s each. (0) COLLECTION OFF THE BOOT PATH, first: retain! and compact-if-due! need the store's sole process, which forbids the warm start of (3), and cost 47-57 s of no service when due. Reuses seon.db/retain!; missing: a sweep that runs beside live readers (a Flow proc on the running root, admitted by the effects owner) or at the stop with the stop's own bound; deps reviews (LMDB, Datahike). (1) RUNTIME-FOR DECIDES (deps reviewed: APPROVED, G slice above): a landing asks (runtime-for main-tree); current is load in place, else restart naming :seon.process/differs; at-load config lines restart, every other line loads. Reuses deps' runtime-for kept by tree (c9cf4ae5c3) and the in-place load (writer main-commit, load-changes!/load-diff!). Measured: main's runtime-for 73k ticks but 1.25 s wall a call; kept, one file read. Expected: of today's 14 restarts, the 9 with no build input become no restart (the reason, once kept, says which). (2) G'S HOLD (flow reviewed; the circular wait fixed): while a restart is owed the :model owner admits no new call and answers restart-when-idle when its calls in flight reach 0; proof seon.fx-admit-test/a-restart-waits-for-every-turn-in-flight (red 14:08). Reuses fx/owner-proc's dispatch; cost O(1) a call, the wait the longest model call in flight. (3) WARM START BESIDE THE OLD: the new binary boots against the same store, preloaded (its fork server warm, load-all!'s id-first path), and the old stops only when the new answers ready, so the gap is the writer handoff, not a boot. Reuses the one start (seon.system/start!), the fork server's preload, the store's per-branch writers beside one LMDB (flow's lockbench: 16 writers 12.4x one). Missing: (a) step 0; (b) main's one writer handed over (old flushes and closes main's writer, new opens it: the one-writer-per-branch rule kept by construction, deps and data review); (c) the api's port handed over (the new binds after the old closes, or a second port the client re-asks). Estimate to measure: the handoff = the old's flush (~15 ms, :fact) + the new's writer open; lower bound one fsync. (4) WORK IN FLIGHT IS FACTS (flow reviews): flights, requests and running evals are facts on main, the new root resumes each at its turn boundary, and agents re-fork from the new binary at their next turn (owner 2026-11-15: no root adopts another's children). Reuses reconcile boot-wakes (reconcile.clj:1345), previous-end (:seon.system/stopped), the agent's branch as its resume point. Missing: (a) a reconciler flight as a fact {task step request} re-issued idempotently (judges and verdicts reuse by digest); (b) a :running task of the old boot parked then woken, never reaped :lost (reconcile.clj:870); (c) prove and :done requests keyed by run-key and task so a client's re-ask after EOF reattaches. (5) THE STOP NEVER OVERRUNS: stop! names each part's ticks beside its wall (:phases, on main 637875cfd6) and writes its fibers first, so the next overrun names its step; the idle waiter measured is the vectors proc's first-round scan (task a-stop-takes-no-wait-it-does-not-need); with (2) and (4) a stop waits only for steps already ending. ORDER: 1 and 2 now (G, this branch), 5 with them, 0 next (it unblocks 3), then 4, then 3. Done when: a pin lands during a watched seon_done and it ends green (the proof above), and a src-only landing restarts nothing. DEPS REVIEW of steps 0 and 3b (2026-11-15 14:5x, read Datahike b7a60b3b and seon.db). (0) retain! must not run beside live agents as it is: its sweep spares what a commit wrote before its head flips by a safe point held IN-PROCESS (datahike gc.cljc:149, gc_guard.cljc: every writer of a database in one JVM), and Seon runs a writer per agent process on one LMDB, so a sweep beside them can delete an agent's half-done commit and leave its head on missing nodes; readers are safe (a held value at a kept commit is never swept). Ways: (a) collect only when root is the store's only process (at a stop, or when no agent lives), never on the boot path; (b) deps' fork makes the safe point store-wide (a commit puts an in-flight marker before its values and drops it after the head flips, the sweep takes the oldest marker, a dead pid's is cleared: one LMDB put pair a commit). compact swaps the data file, so it runs at a stop either way. Runtime's pick: (a) now (90/10: no fork change, and the boot no longer waits 47-57 s), (b) when collection must run while agents live. (3b) The writer handoff is safe strictly ordered: the old root's commit-pending!, it stops writing and releases its connection, then the new root connects (it reads the head again; the writer's last commit comes from the st